-- CONTROL — POSITIVE, and it is what makes every other row in this corpus mean
-- something.
--
-- Structurally this is the 1182 shape with ONE dimension flipped: the two interfaces
-- declare DISTINCT method names (`m1`, `m2`) instead of the same one. Two interfaces,
-- two impls on the same data type, two calls, same declaration shapes -- so the
-- permuter moves exactly as much source as it does on the colliding cases.
--
-- It MUST be invariant, and it is deliberately UNLEDGERED so the gate grades it as
-- such. That is the positive control this instrument needs in both directions:
--
--   * if it ever DIVERGES, the finding is not "interfaces are order-dependent" -- it
--     is that reordering interface declarations perturbs programs with no method-name
--     collision at all, which is a much larger and differently-shaped defect;
--   * if the PERMUTER silently stopped moving anything (wrong chunk regex, a
--     `^interface` that no longer matches after a syntax change), every case in this
--     corpus would read invariant and the ledgered rows would go red as "DRAINED" --
--     a broken instrument reporting a fixed compiler. This row cannot detect that
--     alone, which is why the gate ALSO refuses any case whose permuter finds fewer
--     than two interface declarations, and prints the permutation count per row.
interface C1 a where
  m1 : a -> Int

interface C2 a where
  m2 : a -> Int

data D = D

impl C1 D where
  m1 _ = 1

impl C2 D where
  m2 _ = 2

main = println (m1 D + m2 D)
